# Stage 7 — the hyphenated markup families.
#
# Gravity Forms uses eleven markup prefixes, not one, and this stage covers the
# ten that are safe to move: gform-, gforms-, gf-, gfield_, gfield-, ginput_,
# gsection_, gresults-, gaddon-, gficon- and gchoice_. 3617 identifiers,
# 31049 occurrences — larger than every earlier stage combined, and almost all
# of it is CSS classes in rendered markup plus the stylesheets that select them.
# Same trade as stage 6: custom CSS written against these class names stops
# applying, and an old site is fixed by changing the selector.
#
# Held back:
#
#   * gravity-theme. It is not a class, it is a value — the form theme slug,
#     stored per form in the form meta and in the rg_gforms_default_theme
#     option, and validated with in_array( $slug, array( 'legacy',
#     'gravity-theme', 'orbital' ) ). Renaming it makes every existing form's
#     stored theme fail that check and fall back. It needs a read-side
#     migration, which is its own change.
#   * gform-icons-common, gform-icons-theme, gform-icons-admin,
#     gform-icons-orbital, gform-pagination, gform-image-choice and
#     gform-products, which are asset file names. Renaming the token without
#     renaming the file breaks the enqueue.
#   * The REST namespace. It is gf/v2, reached as /wp-json/gf/v2/..., and the
#     'gf-' literal in class-kdna-rest-authentication.php is the third-party
#     endpoint prefix that goes with it. Both are a public URL; changing them
#     breaks any external integration, so they are left alone deliberately.
#
# The boundary guard treats "-" as extending a token, so a concatenation prefix
# moves with the names built from it: 'gf-notice-' . $type becomes
# 'kdna-notice-' . $type and still matches the renamed .kdna-notice-warning.

